2. CONUS404 dataset
=============================================
If details of Earth's hydroclimate could be resolved down to kilometer-scale spatial resolution and hourly time scales for long time periods, it could revolutionize hydroclimate studies around the world. The increased computer power, better supporting data, and improvements in atmospheric modeling now available make this possible for the conterminous United States (CONUS). Through a collaborative initiative between the U.S. Geological Survey (USGS) and the National Science Foundation National Center for Atmospheric Research (NSF NCAR), a 4-km, 40-plus-year dataset was created to realistically resolve mesoscale atmospheric phenomena, such as convective systems, and to accurately place them onto the hydrologic modeling basins.

CONUS404 is a regional hydroclimate dataset produced using the WRF (Weather Research and Forecasting; Skamarock et al., 2005) model to dynamically downscale the fifth generation European Centre for Medium-Range Weather Forecasts reanalysis (ERA5; Hersbach et al. 2020). ERA5 provides physcially consistent, hourly estimates of temperature, precipitation, and radiation and other dynamic variables that can be used to force and calibrate hydrologic models, but only at 30-km coarser grid spacing. CONUS404 covers the historical period of 1979 to 2024 over the CONUS domain at 4-km spatial resolution and hourly temporal resolution.

This convection-permitting simulation enables realistic representation of orographic precipitation and snowpack over the western U.S. and mesoscale convective precipitation over the central and eastern U.S., both of which are vital water resources for the CONUS (Rasmussen et al., 2023). CONUS404 provides an opportunity to drive hydrological models and conduct water budget analyses at relatively high spatial and temporal resolution, which are important for hydroclimate studies and water availability assessment.



2.1 Domain configuration
---------------------------------------------
The model domain at 4-km horizontal resolution covers the Conterminous U.S., as well as a significant portion of southern Canada and northern Mexico and part of the Eastern Pacific and Wester Atlantic (see :ref:`fig_conus404_domain`). The domain has 1,368 grid points in the east-west direction and 1,016 grid points in the north-south direction yielding a size of 5,472 km x 4,064 km. This large domain ensures that the weather systems that impact the CONUS are well represented.

The model is configured with 51 stretched vertical levels, with the top at 50 hPa. The finest vertical grid spacing is ~ 50 m near the surface, stretching to ~ 700 m near the model top.

